1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,178
A claim can sound convincing.

2
00:00:02,358 --> 00:00:05,302
But does the official voting booklet actually support it?

3
00:00:05,482 --> 00:00:08,575
That is the question behind Apertus Evidence Lab.

4
00:00:09,755 --> 00:00:17,350
The OST challenge asks us to compare a claim with a Swiss voting booklet:
supported, contradicted, or not established.

5
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:20,709
And the claim and document may be in different languages.

6
00:00:21,889 --> 00:00:30,870
Our starting point is a Jev-style approach: keep the language model frozen,
and train a small decision head on its internal representations.

7
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:35,573
Here, Apertus version one point five supplies those representations.

8
00:00:36,753 --> 00:00:39,014
We build the document pipeline around it.

9
00:00:39,194 --> 00:00:48,602
Extract page text, combine keyword and multilingual semantic retrieval, and
pack original passages into an eight-thousand-token context budget.

10
00:00:48,782 --> 00:00:51,363
Production needs only the booklet and the claim.

11
00:00:52,543 --> 00:00:57,087
We separate training and development validation by connected voting events.

12
00:00:57,267 --> 00:01:02,366
We compare context budgets and test what happens with empty or wrong-event
documents.

13
00:01:02,546 --> 00:01:06,365
Performance drops support document dependence, with limitations.

14
00:01:07,545 --> 00:01:15,331
On two hundred seventy-six development examples, the best base configuration
scores point six seven two Macro F-one.

15
00:01:15,511 --> 00:01:19,031
The original full-context head reaches point eight nine four.

16
00:01:19,211 --> 00:01:26,017
Our selected hybrid eight-K system reaches point nine four nine, and point
nine four two across languages.

17
00:01:27,197 --> 00:01:34,727
That selected configuration uses about forty-three percent fewer prompt
tokens than the original full-context system.

18
00:01:34,907 --> 00:01:38,939
These are development results, not the organizer's hidden evaluation.

19
00:01:40,119 --> 00:01:46,903
The interface returns a decision with inspectable source passages, page
numbers, and downloadable results.

20
00:01:47,083 --> 00:01:52,481
Those passages show the source context; they are not claimed as a validated
minimal proof.

21
00:01:53,661 --> 00:01:55,026
Apertus Evidence Lab.

22
00:01:55,206 --> 00:01:56,273
Read the claim.

23
00:01:56,453 --> 00:01:57,541
Check the source.
